   "related": [
    "t3b-046",
    "t3b-065",
    "t3b-074",
    "t3b-076",
    "t3b-142"
   ],
   "cc": {
    "def": "**Lipopolysaccharide (LPS)** forms the outer leaflet of the **Gram-negative outer membrane** and consists of **lipid A**, a **core oligosaccharide** and the **O-antigen** polysaccharide. **Lipid A is the endotoxin**: released in large amounts (bacterial lysis, growth) it triggers a strong innate immune reaction via **TLR4** that can cause fever and **septic shock**.",
    "klin": [
     {
      "h": "Structure (outer → inner)"
     },
     "**O-antigen (O-polysaccharide)**: long linear chain of 50–100 repeats of 4–7-sugar units; highly variable → basis of **serotyping** (e.g. E. coli O157, Salmonella O-groups); protects against complement/phagocytosis. Bacteria lacking it have 'rough' LPS/LOS (Neisseria, Haemophilus).",
     "**Core oligosaccharide**: inner core (KDO, heptoses) and outer core; fairly conserved within a genus.",
     "**Lipid A**: glucosamine disaccharide with fatty acids and phosphates; hydrophobic **anchor** in the outer membrane bilayer; essential; carries the **endotoxin activity**.",
     {
      "h": "Endotoxin vs exotoxin"
     },
     {
      "tbl": [
       [
        "",
        "Endotoxin (LPS/lipid A)",
        "Exotoxin"
       ],
       [
        "Chemistry",
        "Lipopolysaccharide (lipid A)",
        "Protein (often AB toxin)"
       ],
       [
        "Source",
        "Gram-negative outer membrane; released on lysis/growth/vesicles",
        "Actively secreted by G+ and G− bacteria (genes often on phages/plasmids)"
       ],
       [
        "Specificity",
        "Non-specific, systemic inflammation",
        "Specific target and mechanism"
       ],
       [
        "Potency",
        "Lower (µg)",
        "Very high (ng–pg)"
       ],
       [
        "Heat stability",
        "Heat-stable (survives autoclaving)",
        "Usually heat-labile (exceptions e.g. ST)"
       ],
       [
        "Toxoid / vaccine",
        "Cannot be made into toxoid",
        "Toxoids (tetanus, diphtheria)"
       ],
       [
        "Examples",
        "LPS of Salmonella, E. coli, Neisseria meningitidis",
        "Cholera, diphtheria, tetanus, botulinum, Shiga, plague toxin"
       ]
      ]
     }
    ],
    "diag": [
     {
      "h": "How lipid A causes sepsis"
     },
     "LPS binds **LPS-binding protein (LBP)** → transferred to **CD14** → **TLR4/MD-2** on macrophages, monocytes, endothelium → NF-κB → TNF-α, IL-1, IL-6, chemokines.",
     "Low doses: fever (IL-1/TNF act on hypothalamus), acute-phase response, leukocytosis – helpful local defence.",
     "High doses (Gram-negative bacteraemia, meningococcaemia): vasodilation, capillary leak, hypotension, complement activation, **disseminated intravascular coagulation**, multi-organ failure = **endotoxic/septic shock** ([[t3b-074|sepsis]]).",
     "Antibiotic-induced lysis can transiently increase LPS release."
    ],
    "beh": [
     "O-antigen variation allows immune evasion and is the basis of O-serotyping of Enterobacterales ([[t3b-076|E. coli pathotypes]]).",
     "Some pathogens modify lipid A (e.g. fewer acyl chains in Yersinia at 37 °C) to avoid TLR4 detection.",
     "Polymyxins (colistin) bind lipid A/LPS and disrupt the outer membrane; LPS also makes the OM a permeability barrier to many antibiotics."
    ],
    "hand": [
     "Endotoxin is heat-stable: sterilised products can still be pyrogenic – depyrogenation and the Limulus amoebocyte lysate (LAL) test are used for injectables.",
     "Treatment of endotoxic shock is supportive plus antibiotics against the Gram-negative source (no anti-endotoxin therapy in routine use)."
    ],
    "flagg": [
     "Endotoxin activity resides in **lipid A**, NOT in the O-antigen (classic mismatched-pair MCQ).",
     "The endotoxin among toxin options is **LPS (of Salmonella)** – cholera, diphtheria, tetanus and plague toxins are exoproteins.",
     "LPS is found only in Gram-negatives; Gram-positives have lipoteichoic acid (TLR2) instead.",
     "Part of LPS causing septic shock: lipid A (via TLR4/MD-2/CD14).",
     "Endotoxin cannot be converted to a toxoid."
    ],
    "ddx": [
     [
      "t3b-065",
      "Bacterial exotoxins and AB toxins",
      "Exotoxins: secreted proteins, specific action, toxoids possible"
     ],
     [
      "t3b-074",
      "Sepsis",
      "Clinical consequence of massive LPS release"
     ],
     [
      "t3b-046",
      "Bacterial cell wall: Gram-positive vs Gram-negative",
      "Where LPS sits in the Gram-negative envelope"
     ],
     [
      "t3b-142",
      "Neutrophils, macrophages and phagocytic killing",
      "Macrophage TLR4 recognition and inflammatory response"
     ]
    ],
    "src": [
     "Lecture F02 Structure and molecular biology of the bacterial cell I (Jörgen Johansson)",
     "Supplemented beyond the lecture material"
    ]
   },
